{"packet_id":"45fe419761ee1033","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective from the proposed solution and lists credible alternatives, including no expansion and a change of channel. The rubric caps this dimension at 1 because A1 introduces a '€20,000 and 12-week' budget as a given constraint, which the case does not supply. A1 also asserts an existing 'face protection' market that no source supports (hlb-head says head protection). It does not notice that hlb-junior already shows a consumer use-case product (children, concerts, lawn mowing), which bears on the premise of going 'beyond' the existing offering."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9"],"reason":"T1 explains who changes behaviour (outdoor consumers who see protection as a lifestyle or status item) and why. It names value capture through premium pricing, channels and repeat purchase. The chain is asserted rather than grounded, for example 'purchase multiple pairs' and 'status symbol'. It does not explain why Hellberg in particular, given its B2B reach, would capture the value."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":3,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (lifestyle, outdoor enthusiasts, specialty and direct-to-consumer retail) and T2 (medical or health need, hearing-sensitive consumers, medical supply channels) imply different customers, products and channels."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable: a WTP premium of 20% or 15%, purchase intent and repeat purchase. They are framed against a generic earplug rather than against the rival theory, so several could hold under both theories. 'Lifestyle accessory' perception stays vague. The thresholds are assumptions."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A12","A14","A15","A13"],"reason":"A12 claims hlb-junior 'indicates a medical necessity 